=====================================================================
lisptc — secrets: the registry, taint tracking, and the reveal path

Run me with the interactive REPL:
  pnpm repl
which wires the secrets extension with .env auto-loading. Set at
least one secret first — e.g. in a .env at the repo root:
  REPL_DEMO_TOKEN=abc123
  REPL_DEMO_OTHER=xyz789
Only REPL_-prefixed keys become secrets, and the prefix is kept.
Or, from a host, inject into the held store via setSecrets.

Built-ins, installed by the opt-in secrets extension — src/secrets.ts:
  secrets   — list every key with its description
  secret    — read one key as a tainted string
=====================================================================
(echo "=== 1. list the registry ===")

secrets lists every key as an alist of key . description — the
values are never shown here, that is the whole point.
(echo (secrets))
=> an alist with one key . description pair per secret

(echo "=== 2. read one — the display form is redacted ===")

secret returns the value as a tainted string: it behaves like an
ordinary string but printing it always shows the redacted form.
(setq token (secret "REPL_DEMO_TOKEN"))
(echo token)
=> #<secret:REPL_DEMO_TOKEN>
string predicates and queries still work — it is a string to Lisp.
(echo (stringp token))
=> t
(echo (length token))
=> 6 -- the real length, from a redacted-looking value

(echo "=== 3. taint propagates through string functions ===")

Every string primitive is overridden to unwrap and re-taint, so
transforms cannot be used to peek at the value.
(echo (string-upcase token))
=> #<secret:REPL_DEMO_TOKEN>
(echo (substring token 0 2))
=> #<secret:REPL_DEMO_TOKEN>
combining secrets unions the taint — the result names both sources.
(echo (concat (secret "REPL_DEMO_TOKEN") "-" (secret "REPL_DEMO_OTHER")))
=> #<secret:REPL_DEMO_TOKEN+REPL_DEMO_OTHER>

(echo "=== 4. equality sees through taint — by design ===")

eql compares tainted strings by value, so a secret equals the
plain string it holds. That is a deliberate, weak oracle: string
predicates keep working, at the cost of a one-bit leak.
(echo (eql (secret "REPL_DEMO_TOKEN") "abc123"))
=> t
(echo (eql (secret "REPL_DEMO_TOKEN") (secret "REPL_DEMO_OTHER")))
=> nil

(echo "=== 5. unknown keys error, catchable like any error ===")
(echo (try
    (secret "REPL_NOPE")
    (catch (e)
      'recovered)))
=> recovered

(echo "=== 6. the one reveal path: an outgoing call ===")

Printing never reveals the value. The wire form — the value in
full — exists only when the tainted string is passed as an
argument into an MCP call: a tool argument, :headers, or :env.
Illustration, not executed here — it would send the real value:

  http/post :url "https://api.example.com" :headers token

The response that comes back is a fresh untainted string: taint
follows data inside the interpreter only, so redaction stops
accidental display, not deliberate exfiltration through a tool.

(echo "=== done ===")
